#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135006} :activity/id #uuid "e6580470-6b81-57e7-bf68-123e2ad0a485" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050659 :topic/id #uuid "5cfd2722-99a9-5eb4-adb4-6386be397f59" :topic/math-academy-id 2218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135147 :tutorial/content "As we mentioned earlier, rigid motions are **distance-preserving**.\n\nFor example, if a rigid motion maps $\\triangle ABC$ to $\\triangle A'B'C'$, then the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent:\n\n$$\nAB = A'B', \\qquad AC = A'C', \\qquad BC = B'C'\n$$\n\nIn addition, rigid motions are **angle-preserving**, which means that the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent as well:\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = m\\angle A', \\qquad m\\angle B = m\\angle B', \\qquad m\\angle C = m\\angle C'\n$$\n\nSince all of the corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the triangles are congruent, and we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle A'B'C'\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4514dc0f-bb78-52d9-a728-8a09f7cbf667" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6157 :tutorial/title "Further Properties of Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "6caa4091-01f6-57a9-8303-9c7ac2687088" :step/math-academy-id 37342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135148 :tutorial/content "So, remember, there are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nThere are two properties of rigid motions we need to know:\n\n- *A rigid motion maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.*\n- *For any pair of congruent polygons, there exists a rigid motion that maps one polygon onto another.*\n\nConsider the diagram below. Let's use the concept of rigid motions to explain why $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent.\n\n![](images/04/0487c9bb2a640a44e9ee67830937db4272c97f4a5f5101e43e3dcf3edd6c3a1c.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of reflection in the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/18/183b182e26df36c6cfe6a9d682e0f3b000bff40ce7d405255a0abfee8529a946.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal P$ is mapped to $\\mathcal Q$ under a rigid motion (reflection), $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ must be congruent." :tutorial/id #uuid "511ff35e-2666-5989-ad2f-e3e9fe541c7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6163 :tutorial/title "Properties of Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "1b767960-73f6-5f1a-86d5-06e3a151b4d8" :step/math-academy-id 37367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135021 :tutorial/content "A **rigid motion** is any transformation that does not change the distances and angles between points. Rigid motions are also called **isometries**.\n\nThere are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\n**Watch out!** The following types of transformations are *not* rigid motions:\n\n- Dilations\n- Stretches" :tutorial/id #uuid "9208af14-0cfd-5ca9-989f-3bf18330cf12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2294 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "66e47972-9ac9-5e15-abc6-6b2f033b161a" :step/math-academy-id 9555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133976 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133546 :question/id #uuid "25f331e5-db8f-58bd-a4a6-24d7ed09db10" :question/math-academy-id "e-2906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "811ba45c-7467-5cef-9fb8-a8282891b607" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526093}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "43999184-e956-5a86-8726-8697e9473d18" :step/math-academy-id 9556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133547 :question/id #uuid "46db11d7-8448-54f5-a5ea-46abfc746574" :question/math-academy-id "e-2907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe2598e-57bd-53fb-8477-9c4c5a8d3fa4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134463}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polygons That Map to Each Other Under Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "21aa14a0-3abb-5d4a-92b6-d08d24876eb6" :step/math-academy-id 9558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133548 :question/id #uuid "d928641d-fd70-5cbb-a217-e9e454a8bd51" :question/math-academy-id "e-2909"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4ffa315-2f50-51da-a928-329e1816706f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526046}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "971e2526-fd20-58e6-bc97-09ccc8b2617e" :step/math-academy-id 9562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133549 :question/id #uuid "5fb05996-774b-5a89-9cc1-6876cc60338e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050239}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526062}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "a91d4728-a024-5878-9599-76838230c6ed" :step/math-academy-id 9567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134893}}] :activity/title "Rigid Motions and Congruence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134053}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526145} :activity/id #uuid "48cf192b-2c32-50db-af73-daef43ed52b6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050659 :topic/id #uuid "5cfd2722-99a9-5eb4-adb4-6386be397f59" :topic/math-academy-id 2218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e023e0-beb4-5806-8cfe-d70eb0013d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525347} {:answer/id #uuid "46b79831-e3c1-595b-9cb4-6b62ccc50e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525384} {:answer/id #uuid "804bf539-d1b6-549f-b8a5-0e0cbf6bc49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525524} {:answer/id #uuid "9628c0d6-1a6a-5d22-872b-ffa2a9fa9cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525589} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb2bf95-13d6-556d-aa77-d9bd810bd93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1af57a5-5f5f-569f-842d-ee65f2687864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e99174b2-b4df-59a0-af23-9fbd3ca32fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-45606" :question/problem "The polygon ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}{E}^{′}$ is the image of the polygon $ABCDE$ under the transformation $(x,y)↦(y,-x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\nII. $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\nIII. $CD<{C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given transformation is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures. \n\n![](images/9b/9b250f6eda245b8a3530851d21c5438ded3fcbe9ba8cbf6db524a2c2749c3d86.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $CD={C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "77aed4fc-bc17-5249-9c81-e5b8c87275ce"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc2d4c0-1618-5fe7-9246-84ee55b59236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525356} {:answer/id #uuid "45ac97e4-9a4a-51c4-8291-5b3046a11d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525382} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1355da-68ee-5967-9be0-84e4e28a596b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525639} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4641ea-e1d1-5f01-ba5e-a6e9674b75ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525751} {:answer/id #uuid "fec35c2b-ba0b-54c0-a2a3-cfc01929dd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525841} :answer-field/id #uuid "f188ddc0-815b-5c23-abd4-3b93e5f115dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a228790b-494e-5666-af68-646e1b5c23fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-45302" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d89477b4c1f7b976f86d1cc5d77c1729826358e1c1367e7982ccd59970c2ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the following combinations of transformations map the triangle $ABC$ to a congruent triangle?\n\nI. A reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a rotation of ${60}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the point $A.$\n\nII. A translation of $2$ units up, followed by a reflection in the line $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\nIII. A rotation of ${15}^{∘}$ clockwise about a given point $B,$ followed by a translation of $3$ units up." :question/worked-solution "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Combination I maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a reflection, followed by a rotation) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination II maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a translation, followed by a reflection) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination III maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a rotation, followed by a translation) are rigid motions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "b5ba0576-4e3a-591c-8ecc-77275a7095f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d2332-fb73-5b0c-ad16-9c15881f170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under rotation by ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525215} {:answer/id #uuid "419e2a9c-1e02-5f01-b0c6-b4337088507d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under reflection in the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525368} {:answer/id #uuid "b31b218d-73a7-50ba-b9cf-fe3a8e544a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The polygons are not congruent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525665} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bbbff6-f856-5bda-a992-b2ea7e00eb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525804} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d4e61b-9cb2-5b59-b64f-f3a8558b9a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under translation by $5$ units to the right and $3$ units up" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525804} :answer-field/id #uuid "72cf26f9-bb61-5a25-a7c9-0dc42775de30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fb7b6f3-9396-53a9-a0b3-7b8a7a56bc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-45177" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d77d6d9dda343a95b44fa60afbfee68619aa59bf9c6ba70eb229d072bf9a73.png)\n\nIn terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $P$ and $Q$ congruent?" :question/worked-solution "The polygons $P$ and $Q$ are congruent because $Q$ is the image of $P$ under the action of translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down.\n\n![](images/15/15120f2beae5043dd439f62bbc376f9b660ec97cf2541c5e2cb28e363ceed202.png)"} :step/id #uuid "dfbf2384-6d9e-556e-a5aa-220fee8fa0e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526139}}] :activity/title "Rigid Motions and Congruence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525198}]}
